t the dollar is is strong at the  minute but this is about more than just  the strength  let's get back to borrowing rates  because uk government borrows at a  cheaper rate than the us government also  the maturity of our debt is twice as  long as the maturity of german  government debt so that puts us in a  very good position also bear in mind  that as i said if the inflation outlook  improves that improves the borrowing  figures as well what comes back to this  is that the markets want to have  confidence about the policy no one  should ever be complacent about markets  i've been in markets for 30 years but  it's about explaining clearly what you  want to do so coming back to your  question on the energy crisis markets i  think will be reassured on that front  making the bank of england really take  stock of inflation is reassuring but  then the markets i think will take  comfort from a fiscal policy that's not  just going to stabilize the economy but  it's quite clear that debt to gdp which  is lower in the uk than the other g7  countries is on the clear downward trend  so it's fiscal discipline fiscal  financial solvency but the market's  clearly like it with anyone they'll have  to see what is actually unveiled and  that's the key thing it's pretty clear  that you are uh happy excited even to  see some of this trust's policies and  going forward um she said that she wants  to set up a panel of economic advisers  to help steer her policy are you going  to be on it well she said that the  weekend i don't know obviously is up to  the prime minister who's on it but he  would say yes  well depends on the terms of course but  the answer is probably yes but the  important thing about who who is on it  is obviously important but we need to  confront treasury orthodoxy since 2008  trend growth in the uk has been very low  the treasury says  growth is low more the budget deficit is  structural a decade ago they wrongly  used that economic thinking to justify  austerity which was wrong and in the  last two years they used the same  economic thinking to justify tax  increases what liz trust is saying let's  not be in a hole and dig deeper let's  actually ask why we can't get stronger  growth let's get stronger growth higher  wages and sustain what is pretty good  jobs picture at the moment so let's see  what she does i do think it's a sea  change in thinking and as we've seen  today she's quite she believes in it i  think the markets actually will give her  a vote of confidence if she does deliver  on what she has said oh well we'll find  out won't we in the months ahead thank  you so much for being on the program out  there jared lyons
 

